CORE
main.c
--32 --unwind 9 --rounds 3 --por --array-rf-bytes --allow-pointer-unsoundness
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
A 65-byte array is above the splitting bound, so a store to one element
rebuilds the whole array from this thread view of it. With many accesses
--array-rf declines it by its cost model, and before the fix the two halves
written by the two threads could erase each other: a false violation. The
store now reads and writes the array in an atomic section of its own, as it
already did for arrays of symbolic size. Fails on a binary without the fix.
